  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Improved Governance</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">During the reporting period, Gov4Res asissted the Ministry of Finance in Fiji to roll out a new Chart of Accounts that incoporates gender and climate change. This is a significant achievement as the Government of Fiji is the first in the region to do so, initiating a critical step in the establishment of a national climate change expenditure tracking system. 
&#x200d;
Gov4Res has continued to work closely with the Fijian Ministry of Rural and Maritime Development and Disaster Management which is now implementing upwards of 150 gender and socially inclusive, risk-informed community projects each budget cycle using the updated planning budgeting systems. These projects include community access and farm roads, bridges, footpaths, water infrastructure and housing.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Climate adaptation, mitigation, environment and biodiversity</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">During this reporting period, Australia&#x2019;s financial contribution to Gov4Res supported significant progress in the review and adoption of the Climate Change Typology (CCT) by the Ministry of Finance, Fiji. The design options for the Climate Expenditure tracking system based on the CCT were also completed. 
&#x200d;
The Ministry of Finance&apos;s Budget division has recently acquired a new information system called Questica, which will streamline and automate the budget processes and climate tagging.  
&#x200d;
The Gov4Res program supported the recruitment of five positions during the reporting period, two in the climate change division, two in the strategic planning office and one in the budget division to assist government on climate budget planning and tagging. Gov4 Res is assisting the Government to create and fund these positions, that will later be absorbed by the Government.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Climate adaptation, mitigation, environment and biodiversity</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Vanuatu Government with CFAN adviser Andres Toro Runruil&apos;s support successfully secured funding from Government of Australia&#x2019;s REnew Pacific fund for the Empowering Rural Communities of Vanuatu through Renewable Energy Solutions (Solar PV for Water, Education and Health Centers) project. This project has been in the CFAN Vanuatu pipeline for about a year. Toro Runruil was also instrumental in securing co financing for this work from the UK government, which played a role in securing the overall funding from REnew Pacific. This project contributes to Vanuatu&apos;s vision of transitioning toward a sustainable and resilient energy future. The undertaking aims to provide electricity to rural remote off-grid communities to provide water security, quality education, and health services. It is estimated that it will benefit more than 5,000 people in eight rural communities. Toro Runruil played a central role in building on GGGI&#x2019;s earlier work under Solar Water Pump Phases I and II, positioning this new initiative as a natural and scalable Phase III. By building on existing studies, system designs, and community consultations, the advisor was able to present a well-developed, shovel-ready proposal that aligned perfectly with REnew Pacific&#x2019;s requirements for implementation-ready projects by experienced partners.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
